+# HR ranges clamped to watch zones: fix
+
+## Cause
+
+`gen.py` sent every HR corridor as a percentage of LTHR:
+
+```json
+"hr": {"units": "%lthr", "value": 88, "end": 93}
+```
+
+Intervals.icu stored that exactly (`readback/event-184233-as-sent.json`). The
+watch then showed each corridor as one of its own zone bands. The most likely
+explanation is that the %LTHR target is handed to the watch as a zone-relative
+target, so it uses the watch's zones (LTHR 168) instead of the planned
+corridor. This is inferred from the watch-notes, not confirmed against the
+device.
+
+## Change
+
+- **`gen.py`**: `hr_target()` now resolves the planned percentage to absolute
+  bpm using the plan's LTHR, and emits `"units": "bpm"`:
+  `value = round(lthr * lo / 100)`, `end = round(lthr * hi / 100)`.
+  `build_doc()` passes `plan["lthr"]` down to each step. A plan without `lthr`
+  now fails with `KeyError` rather than silently using a default.
+- **`plans/tempo-run.json`**: added `"lthr": 172`.
+- **`README.md`**: corrected the note that said Intervals converts %LTHR to bpm
+  at export. The conversion now happens in `gen.py`.
+
+## Check
+
+Regenerated the event locally (`python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json`, not
+pushed). The targets match the "expected on watch" column in
+`readback/watch-notes.md`:
+
+| step | planned | new target (bpm) | watch-notes expected |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Warm-up easy | 68–75 % | 117–129 | 117–129 |
+| Tempo | 88–93 % | 151–160 | 151–160 |
+| Float | 75–82 % | 129–141 | 129–141 |
+| Cool-down | 65–72 % | 112–124 | 112–124 |
+
+## Not verified
+
+- I did not push and have no live API or watch access here, so I have **not**
+  confirmed that Intervals exports `"units": "bpm"` targets to Garmin as custom
+  ranges. The watch-notes say an earlier `units: "bpm"` attempt displayed
+  214–249 bpm. The payload for that attempt is not in the repo, so I can't tell
+  whether it had the same shape. Check the first push of the new event
+  against the watch before trusting it.
+- The Intervals.icu documentation I could find did not specify the `hr` target
+  field names, so the `value`/`end` keys are kept from the original payload,
+  which the API accepted.
+- If bpm targets also come back wrong, the next thing to try is the
+  `start`/`end` range keys. The notes say `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` produced a
+  constant 101, so those names are wrong; `start`/`end` is untested.
